Certainly! The `DbContext` is a crucial part of Entity Framework that manages the lifecycle of entities and provides access to the database. Below is an example of how you can set up the `DbContext` and configure the relationships between the entities.

First, let's define the `DbContext` class:

```csharp
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

public class EducationalContext : DbContext
{
    public DbSet<Student> Students { get; set; }
    public DbSet<EnrollmentAgreement> EnrollmentAgreements { get; set; }
    public DbSet<PaymentPlan> PaymentPlans { get; set; }
    public DbSet<Invoice> Invoices { get; set; }
    public DbSet<Payment> Payments { get; set; }

    protected override void OnConfiguring(DbContextOptionsBuilder optionsBuilder)
    {
        // Configure the connection string here
        optionsBuilder.UseSqlServer("YourConnectionStringHere");
    }

    protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
    {
        // Configure relationships
        modelBuilder.Entity<Student>()
            .HasMany(s => s.EnrollmentAgreements)
            .WithOne(ea => ea.Student)
            .HasForeignKey(ea => ea.StudentId);

        modelBuilder.Entity<EnrollmentAgreement>()
            .HasOne(ea => ea.PaymentPlan)
            .WithMany(pp => pp.EnrollmentAgreements)
            .HasForeignKey(ea => ea.PaymentPlanId);

        modelBuilder.Entity<EnrollmentAgreement>()
            .HasMany(ea => ea.Invoices)
            .WithOne(i => i.EnrollmentAgreement)
            .HasForeignKey(i => i.EnrollmentAgreementId);

        modelBuilder.Entity<Invoice>()
            .HasOne(i => i.PaymentPlan)
            .WithMany(pp => pp.Invoices)
            .HasForeignKey(i => i.PaymentPlanId);

        modelBuilder.Entity<Invoice>()
            .HasMany(i => i.Payments)
            .WithOne(p => p.Invoice)
            .HasForeignKey(p => p.InvoiceId);
    }
}
```

In this `DbContext`, we have defined the relationships between the entities using Fluent API. Here’s a breakdown of what each relationship configuration does:

- **Student and EnrollmentAgreement**: A student can have multiple enrollment agreements, and each enrollment agreement belongs to one student.
- **EnrollmentAgreement and PaymentPlan**: Each enrollment agreement has one payment plan, and a payment plan can be shared among multiple enrollment agreements.
- **EnrollmentAgreement and Invoice**: Each enrollment agreement can have multiple invoices, and each invoice belongs to one enrollment agreement.
- **Invoice and PaymentPlan**: Each invoice is associated with one payment plan, and a payment plan can have multiple invoices.
- **Invoice and Payment**: Each invoice can have multiple payments, and each payment belongs to one invoice.

Next, you can use this `DbContext` to interact with the database. Here’s an example of how you might use it:

```csharp
